Partner...this controller has some good structure, but I found 8 issues that should be corrected 
before we proceed. Some are bugs that will definitely cause runtime errors. 
The good news is they're straightforward to fix.

🔴 1. selectedIds is not defined (Critical)
You repeatedly use:

description: `${selectedIds.length} users changed to User(s).`
But selectedIds does not exist anywhere in this controller. 


It should be:

description: `${userIds.length} users changed to User(s).`
Replace every occurrence of:

selectedIds.length
with:

userIds.length
🔴 2. Wrong Model Used During Bulk Delete (Critical)
In the "delete" case you have:

await Prediction.deleteMany({
    _id: { $in: userIds }
});
There is no Prediction model imported in this file, and this is the User Controller.

It should be:

await User.deleteMany({
    _id: { $in: userIds }
});
🔴 3. Expired Action Updates to Active (Logic Bug)
Your "expired" case currently does:

subscriptionStatus: "Active"
but the message says:

Expired
These contradict each other. 


It should be:

subscriptionStatus: "Expired"
provided "Expired" is one of the allowed enum values in your User schema.

🔴 4. role and subscriptionStatus Undefined in deleteUser
In deleteUser you log:

description:
`${user.username} → Role: ${role}, Subscription: ${subscriptionStatus}`
But role and subscriptionStatus are not defined in this function. 


It should use the values from the user being deleted:

description:
`${user.username} → Role: ${user.role}, Subscription: ${user.subscriptionStatus}`
🟡 5. Unused Variables
You destructure:

let { userIds, action, role, subscriptionStatus } = req.body;
But role and subscriptionStatus are never used in bulkAction.

This can simply be:

let { userIds, action } = req.body;
🟡 6. Action Names Must Match the Frontend
Your switch expects values like:

"user"
"superuser"
"admin"
"inactive"
"active"
"expired"
"delete"
Make sure your <select> in users.ejs uses exactly these values. 
If the frontend uses "User" or "Admin" (capitalized), the switch will fall into the default case.

🟡 7. Protect the Last Superuser in Bulk Delete
Your single delete prevents deleting the last superuser, but bulk delete does not.

If someone selects the only superuser and chooses Delete Selected, this controller will delete it.

You should add a similar safeguard before User.deleteMany().

🟢 8. Good Things
These parts are solid:

